\label{def:4.1}
    Let $n \geq 1$ be an integer. We fix a horizontal line in the plane and $n$ distinct points on it. A weight of type $n$ is a sequence of $k \; {\mkern.5mu\begin{tikzpicture}[x=.27em,y=.45em,decoration={markings,mark=at position 0.99 with {\arrow[black]{Stealth[length=4.8pt]}}}]
\draw[color=white,postaction={decorate}] (0,-.1) -- (0,.1);
\end{tikzpicture}\mkern.5mu}$'s and $(n-k) \; {\mkern.5mu\begin{tikzpicture}[x=.27em,y=.45em,decoration={markings,mark=at position 0.99 with {\arrow[black]{Stealth[length=4.8pt]}}}]
\draw[color=white,postaction={decorate}] (0,.1) -- (0,-.1);
\end{tikzpicture}\mkern.5mu}$'s, that we can place on the fixed points on the line. We will denote by $\Delta_n^0$ the set of weights of type $n$ with an even number of ${\mkern.5mu\begin{tikzpicture}[x=.27em,y=.45em,decoration={markings,mark=at position 0.99 with {\arrow[black]{Stealth[length=4.8pt]}}}]
\draw[color=white,postaction={decorate}] (0,-.1) -- (0,.1);
\end{tikzpicture}\mkern.5mu}$'s, and $\Delta_n^1$ the set of weights of type $n$ with an odd number of ${\mkern.5mu\begin{tikzpicture}[x=.27em,y=.45em,decoration={markings,mark=at position 0.99 with {\arrow[black]{Stealth[length=4.8pt]}}}]
\draw[color=white,postaction={decorate}] (0,-.1) -- (0,.1);
\end{tikzpicture}\mkern.5mu}$'s. These sets are called blocks.

\label{def:4.3}
    Let $n \geq 1$ be an integer, we fix a horizontal line in the plane and $n$ distinct points on it. A cup diagram consists of a diagram with cups and vertical lines under the fixed horizontal line, and attached to the fixed points on it. There cannot be intersections, and each cup and line can be decorated or not, with a dot, following the conditions :
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Every decorated cup or line cannot be to the right of a vertical line. 
        \item Every decorated cup cannot be nested in another cup.
    \end{itemize}
    Visually, these conditions tell us that any dot from a decorated arc can be connected with the left boundary of the diagram without intersecting any other arc, if we see the cup diagram in a rectangle with top edge lying on the fixed line.

\label{ex:4.4}
    The following visual example will help to understand Definition~\ref{def:4.3}. Let $n = 7$, the following diagram is a cup diagram because, as we said in the definition, each dot can be connected with the left boundary of the rectangle.
