\label{def:4.17}
    Let $\lambda, \mu \in \Delta_n^p$ be weights, and assume that $\underline{\lambda}\overline{\mu}$ can be oriented. Then we define $I_{\underline{\lambda}\overline{\mu}}$ to be the ideal in $\Bbbk[X_i \ \vert \ 1 \leq i \leq n ]$ generated by
    \begin{itemize}
        \item $X_i^2$ for all $1 \leq i \leq n$.
        \item $X_i - X_j$ if $i$ and $j$ are connected by an undecorated cup or cap in $\underline{\lambda}\overline{\mu}$.
        \item $X_i + X_j$ if $i$ and $j$ are connected by a decorated cup or cap in $\underline{\lambda}\overline{\mu}$.
        \item $X_i$ if $i$ is the endpoint of a half-line in $\underline{\lambda}\overline{\mu}$.
    \end{itemize}
    Then we also define
    $$\mathcal{M}(\underline{\lambda}\overline{\mu}) = \Bbbk[X_i \ \vert \ 1 \leq i \leq n ]/I_{\underline{\lambda}\overline{\mu}}$$

