    $$\chi' : \Bbbk[X_i \ \vert \ 1 \leq i \leq n ] \otimes \Bbbk[X_i \ \vert \ 1 \leq i \leq n ] \to \Bbbk[X_i \ \vert \ 1 \leq i \leq n ] $$
    associated to our surgery procedure. To obtain the image of $f \otimes g \in \Bbbk[X_i \ \vert \ 1 \leq i \leq n ] \otimes \Bbbk[X_i \ \vert \ 1 \leq i \leq n ]$, we will apply the following algorithm :
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item After drawing $a$ below $b$, define $y = f \cdot g \in \Bbbk[X_i \ \vert \ 1 \leq i \leq n ]$.
        \item After connecting all open arcs at same positions, multiply $y$ with $\prod_{C \in \mathcal{C}_{\text{clock}}^{\text{new}}} X_{t(C)}$ where $\mathcal{C}_{\text{clock}}^{\text{new}}$ is the set of all clockwise circles created by this step of connection of open arcs, and $t(C)$ is the position of the rightmost point of the fixed horizontal line which $C$ goes through.
        \item For each surgery, consult the following table 

        \textbf{Merge :} If the surgery produces one connected component out of two, leave $y$ as it is.

        \textbf{Split :} If the surgery produces two connected component out of one, then multiply $y$ with
        \[
\begin{dcases}
(X_i + X_j) & \text{if the surgery occurs at positions } i \text{ and } j \text{,}\\
             & \text{and either the surgery is undotted,} \\
             & \text{or dotted but non-nested} \\[1em]
(X_i - X_j) & \text{if the surgery occurs at positions } i \text{ and } j \text{,}\\
             & \text{and the surgery is dotted and nested with} \\
