Observe first that any square in $\Gamma_n$ has a lowest weight $\lambda$ with two ascending arrows starting from it in the square, because otherwise it would create a cycle that would contradicts the order from~\ref{def:5.6}. We will denote by $\nu$ and $\nu'$ the two weights in the square obtained from $\lambda$, and we can suppose without loss of generality that $\vert \nu \vert \geq \vert \nu' \vert$. We will denote by $\mu$ the fourth weight completing the square. \\
    Observe that $\vert \mu \vert \neq \vert \nu \vert, \vert \nu' \vert$ because $\Gamma_n$ is bipartite, by parity of the heights. Observe also that thanks to Lemma~\ref{lem:5.9}, we cannot have that $\vert \mu \vert < \vert \nu' \vert \leq \vert \nu \vert$. Thus a square in $\Gamma_n$ can only have one of the following two shapes
    $$\begin{tikzcd}[column sep=tiny]
	& \mu & \\
	\nu && {\nu'} \\
	& \lambda \\
	& {\vert \nu' \vert \leq \vert \nu \vert < \vert \mu \vert}
	\arrow[no head,very thick, from=2-1, to=1-2]
	\arrow[no head,very thick, from=2-1, to=3-2]
	\arrow[no head,very thick, from=2-3, to=1-2]
	\arrow[no head,very thick, from=3-2, to=2-3]
    \end{tikzcd} \qquad \text{ or } \qquad  \begin{tikzcd}[column sep=tiny, row sep = scriptsize]
	\nu & \\
	& \mu \\
	& {\nu'} \\
	\lambda \\
	{\vert \nu' \vert < \vert \mu \vert < \vert \nu \vert}
	\arrow[no head,very thick, from=2-2, to=1-1]
	\arrow[no head,very thick, from=3-2, to=2-2]
	\arrow[curve={height=-24pt}, no head,very thick, from=4-1, to=1-1]
	\arrow[no head,very thick, from=4-1, to=3-2]
    \end{tikzcd} $$
