Let $Q$ be any finite quiver, let $I\subset kQ$ be any two-sided ideal and let $R$ be any reduction system satisfying the diamond condition for $I$. Then there is an equivalence of deformation problems between
\begin{enumerate}
    \item deformations of the associative algebra $A=kQ/I$,
    \item deformations of the ideal $I$,
    \item deformations of the reduction system $R$.
\end{enumerate}

We need to show that the above relations are orthogonal to the relations from Theorem~\ref{thm:5.15}. The dual monomial relations are clearly orthogonal to the square and vertices relations, they're also orthogonal to the monomial relations because they correspond to different paths from Lemma~\ref{lem:5.11}. \\
    The dual parallel relations are clearly orthogonal to the monomials and vertices relations, they're also orthogonal to the square relations because the signs compensate. \\
    Finally, the dual relations at vertices are clearly orthogonal to the monomial and square relations, but it's less obvious for the relations at vertices. Fix $\lambda \in \Delta_n^0$, and let $\overline{y}_{\kappa_j}^\lambda$ be the ascending arrows in $\overline{Q}_n$ ending at $e_\lambda$, let also $\mu_i$ for $1 \leq i \leq \text{def}(\lambda)$ be the weights obtained from $\lambda$ by exchanging a ${\mkern.5mu\begin{tikzpicture}[x=.27em,y=.45em,decoration={markings,mark=at position 0.99 with {\arrow[black]{Stealth[length=4.8pt]}}}]
\draw[color=white,postaction={decorate}] (0,.1) -- (0,-.1);
\end{tikzpicture}\mkern.5mu} \cdots {\mkern.5mu\begin{tikzpicture}[x=.27em,y=.45em,decoration={markings,mark=at position 0.99 with {\arrow[black]{Stealth[length=4.8pt]}}}]
\draw[color=white,postaction={decorate}] (0,-.1) -- (0,.1);
\end{tikzpicture}\mkern.5mu}$ or a ${\mkern.5mu\begin{tikzpicture}[x=.27em,y=.45em,decoration={markings,mark=at position 0.99 with {\arrow[black]{Stealth[length=4.8pt]}}}]
\draw[color=white,postaction={decorate}] (0,-.1) -- (0,.1);
\end{tikzpicture}\mkern.5mu} \cdots {\mkern.5mu\begin{tikzpicture}[x=.27em,y=.45em,decoration={markings,mark=at position 0.99 with {\arrow[black]{Stealth[length=4.8pt]}}}]
\draw[color=white,postaction={decorate}] (0,-.1) -- (0,.1);
\end{tikzpicture}\mkern.5mu}$ pair lying in a circle of $e_\lambda$. Then we have
\begin{align*}
    \left\langle
y^{\lambda}_{\kappa_k} x^{\lambda}_{\kappa_k}
-
\sum_i d^{\mu_i}_{\kappa_k}(\lambda) x^{\mu_i}_{\lambda} y^{\mu_i}_{\lambda},
\bar y^{\mu_l}_{\lambda} \bar x^{\mu_l}_{\lambda}
+
\sum_j d^{\mu_l}_{\kappa_j}(\lambda) \bar x^{\lambda}_{\kappa_j} \bar y^{\lambda}_{\kappa_j}
\right\rangle
&=
\sum_j \delta_{\kappa_k,\kappa_j} d^{\mu_l}_{\kappa_j}
-
\sum_i \delta_{\mu_i,\mu_l} d^{\mu_i}_{\kappa_k} \\
&=0
\end{align*}
where we used that
\[
\langle
y^{\lambda}_{\kappa_k} x^{\lambda}_{\kappa_k},
\;
\bar x^{\lambda}_{\kappa_j} \bar y^{\lambda}_{\kappa_j}
\rangle
=
\delta_{\kappa_k,\kappa_j}
\quad \text{ and } \quad
\langle
x^{\mu_i}_{\lambda} y^{\mu_i}_{\lambda},
\;
\bar y^{\mu_l}_{\lambda} \bar x^{\mu_l}_{\lambda}
\rangle
=
\delta_{\mu_i,\mu_l}
\]
This verifies that the dual relations at vertices are indeed orthogonal to the relations at vertices.

